// THE WINDOWS TARGET, 32-BIT (ADR 0122): the win32 base's i686 member, the
// fibre over Win32 for 32-bit Windows (TinyRetroPad's, 6c and 6d).
//
//   glue win32     the base: Win32 over T_LLVM[Σ_OS] (targets/win32), glued
//   model.oro      the data model's set names, ILP32 (D1)
//   space.oro      the address space, 2³¹ bytes (D3)
//   x86.oro        the ISA's own operations at 32 bits: divl, the runtime's (D4)
//
// The double word's 2-by-1 division and add-with-carry are x86-64's, and
// i686's own for K(ℕ*) come with its base at 2³² (D2). Without them the double
// word's operations are LLVM's target-independent ones, which the triple
// expands.
glue win32

// THE TRIPLE (ADR 0093 item 5): 32-bit Windows, MSVC's ABI. A WINAPI function
// is `__stdcall`, x86_stdcallcc, decorated _F@n (ADR 0094 item 3).
triple "i686-pc-windows-msvc"

// THE ADDRESS SPACE BOUNDS EVERY TABLE (ADR 0096, 0122 item 3): a 32-bit
// process's user space is 2³¹ bytes, and a table of n one-byte elements takes
// n + 9 of them, so n ≤ 2³¹ − 9.
fact max_len (a ∈ Array(A)) len(a) <= (2^31-9)

// THE TRIPLE'S RUNTIME (ADR 0123): LLVM's i686 code generator calls __alldiv,
// __aulldiv, __allrem and __aullrem for a 64-bit division it does not expand.
// lib/rt/i686.oro defines them over divl, and every program links
// lib/rt/i686.ll, which `bash gauntlet/regen.sh` writes from it.
runtime "rt/i686.ll"

build "cmd /c .\\build.bat"
